        "In the black stone of the lobby floor you try to put Rivers back on. The left-side smile. The unremarkable shoulders. The badge as furniture. It almost takes. Almost is still the expensive word. Your mouth knows too many rooms to be a liaison's mouth, and the fountain's polite pulse sounds like a metronome for a walk you have not practiced enough.\n\nReception does not store you. That is a gift and an insult. You sign nothing. Helix has already signed you, twice now, once as a tasting and once as an invoice. The citrus humidifiers have been turned back up for the night shift, kindness as climate, as if kindness could wash a lens. A night clerk polishes a fountain edge that is already clean. You envy the simple work for one stupid second, then you put the envy down.",
        "You pause under the sculptural planter that hid a camera on your first evening walk and you do not look into its throat. Looking would be a hello. You give it a civilian's boredom and you step into the scheduled rain.\n\nA pair of analysts argue about gait latency on the steps, the same argument as morning, or its twin. The city recycles conversations the way it recycles weather. You walk around them. Your coat drinks the grid's melancholy and becomes, again, the cheap kind of expensive. Somewhere above, fifty-one is still lit. You do not look up. Looking up is how people stay in a building after the building has finished with the hour.\n\nThe public tram stop is brighter than the private curb. You choose it. Choice, tonight, is mostly about which door you enter. The private curb keeps idling behind you, a black suggestion you have already refused. Refusal, practiced twice in one evening, is starting to feel like a muscle. You do not trust the feeling. Muscles here get rewritten.",
