        "If you lie, she hears the lie and files it beside the last one. If you are honest, she hears Crowe in your vowels and hates a man she has not been allowed to shoot. Either way the call ends before it becomes a comfort. Comfort is how handlers get sloppy and assets get loyal, and Crowe wrote that down in a folio you stole.\n\nYou put the phone away. The plate edge is wet. Your mouth, if you close your eyes, is still a place a director used to sign a file. You open your eyes. The hive is waiting. So is, somewhere under it, a clinician who will not leer. You step toward the tram as {{name}}, which is still a name, which is still a lever, which is still, for this hour, yours to walk with. The clinic light on thirty-eight is the dream you have had since archive nights. You ride past it like a person who still has a stop left.",
